---
import CopyCommand from "../../components/CopyCommand.astro";
import DocMedia from "../../components/DocMedia.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { installCommand, podmanInstallCommand } from "../../lib/site";
import dashboardShot from "../../../mypaas-dashboard-screenshots/2026-08-13T14-22-46-474Z-focused/desktop-dark-00-projects-dashboard.png";
---
<DocsLayout title="Installation" description="Prepare a Linux VM, GitHub OAuth, Cloudflare Tunnel, and complete the guided MyPaaS installer.">
<section><p class="overline">Quick start</p><h2>Choose the container engine</h2><p>Automatic dependency preparation targets fresh Ubuntu or Debian hosts. Docker Engine is the default path; rootful Podman is supported through the Docker-compatible command and socket contract.</p><CopyCommand command={installCommand} label="Docker Engine" /><CopyCommand command={podmanInstallCommand} label="Podman" /></section>
<section><h2>1. Prepare the domain</h2><p>Choose the hostname for the MyPaaS dashboard, for example <code>mypaas.example.com</code>. Project routes use hostnames below the configured public domain, so prepare both the root route and a wildcard route.</p><pre><code>mypaas.example.com
*.mypaas.example.com</code></pre></section>
<section><h2>2. Prepare GitHub OAuth</h2><p>Create a GitHub OAuth application for the MyPaaS instance. The homepage uses the instance URL and the callback ends with <code>/api/auth/github/callback</code>.</p><DocMedia title="GitHub OAuth application" source="GitHub" note="vendor UI · capture pending" placeholder="Replace with a verified GitHub OAuth App form capture. Show the homepage and callback fields; keep all credentials hidden." /><p><a href="/docs/github-oauth/">Detailed GitHub OAuth setup →</a></p></section>
<section><h2>3. Prepare Cloudflare Tunnel</h2><p>Create a Cloudflare Tunnel for the MyPaaS host and configure published application routes for the dashboard hostname and wildcard project hostname, both reaching the MyPaaS Caddy service.</p><DocMedia title="Cloudflare Tunnel and published routes" source="Cloudflare" note="vendor UI · capture pending" placeholder="Replace with verified Cloudflare captures for the tunnel and both published application routes. Hide account-specific identifiers." /><aside class="note"><p>MyPaaS runs its own <code>cloudflared</code> container in the production stack; avoid configuring a duplicate permanent tunnel process for the same MyPaaS instance.</p></aside><p><a href="/docs/cloudflare-tunnel/">Detailed Cloudflare Tunnel setup →</a></p></section>
<section><h2>4. Run the bootstrap</h2><p>The bootstrap prepares the selected engine path, checks out MyPaaS, and starts the browser setup wizard. The wizard stays on the VM loopback interface and bootstrap provides a protected temporary access path or an SSH forwarding fallback.</p></section>
<section><h2>5. Complete the installer</h2><p>The wizard collects the instance domain, owner identity, GitHub OAuth application values, Cloudflare Tunnel value, and production configuration required to start the control plane.</p><DocMedia title="MyPaaS Install Wizard" source="MyPaaS" note="product UI · capture pending" placeholder="Replace with the monochrome Install Wizard screenshot after capturing it from a clean test VM. Use example values only." /><ol class="steps"><li>Confirm the public domain.</li><li>Configure the owner GitHub identity.</li><li>Enter the GitHub OAuth application values.</li><li>Enter the Cloudflare Tunnel value.</li><li>Review the generated production configuration and start installation.</li></ol></section>
<section><h2>6. Production topology</h2><p>The production control plane runs PostgreSQL, the Go API, SvelteKit dashboard, Caddy, and cloudflared. When enabled and supported, the installer also installs the pinned <code>mypaas-statd</code> release as a host-native systemd service.</p></section>
<section><h2>7. Verify the installation</h2><pre><code>cd ~/MyPaas
bash scripts/verify-production.sh</code></pre><p>Verify API readiness, Caddy control access, expected network topology, configured statd availability, and both root and wildcard public routes.</p><DocMedia src={dashboardShot} alt="MyPaaS projects dashboard after installation" title="Expected dashboard state" source="MyPaaS dashboard" note="captured 13 Aug 2026" /></section>
<section><h2>8. Deploy the first project</h2><p>Once verification passes, continue with the source-first project workflow.</p><p><a href="/docs/first-deployment/">First deployment →</a></p></section>
<section><h2>Moving an existing installation?</h2><p>Use the supported VM migration flow instead of changing container engines in place. See <a href="/docs/migration/">Migration</a>.</p></section>
</DocsLayout>
